being in prison what it does to your  mental health  it has a devastating effect on it  it makes you feel weak  makes you feel sad it makes you feel  happy it makes you feel all different  type of emotions  and sometimes you can't escape those  emotions  it's a familiar story  shortly after being expelled from school  stefan fell into the wrong crowd  landing himself in prison  but despite being diagnosed with autism  and adhd his needs were never addressed  and his mental health started to  deteriorate there's violence there's  noise  this one with autism that's a that's a  very bad place to put them noise lies  everything's going on at once  so it's like every day you're having a  sensory overload but to them it's a sign  of disobedience bad behavior  because of  because of the lack of understanding  and eventually he began to self-harm  i got scars on my arm i called him  choices between life and death he isn't  alone  in 2020 for every 1 000 prisoners there  were  691 incidents of self-harm  and a recent report into the criminal  justice system  found there are failings at every stage  there has been too little progress over  the last  10 years there's been a particular  failure to pass information between  different parts of the system  and we're seeing an acute shortage of  mental health hospital beds and very  long waits for community services as  well donna and her family know this all  too well  her brother tommy took his own life  whilst in prison  but she says before this the warning  signs were loud and clear he was cutting  his face  he tried to set fire to his cell  um  and he had his mental health  deteriorated very significantly  following his move to segregation  and he was given absolutely no access to  any mental health support during those  three days that he was in segregation on  the lead up to his death the inspectors  are calling for major improvements  across the system  starting with a common definition of  mental ill health to ensure that all  those charged are able to understand and  participate in the criminal justice  process  shaman freeman powell sky news
 

